/* Stable story IDs and verse-coordinate episode IDs. */
const bibleStories=[{"id":90000,"title":"The Story of a Beautiful Garden","subtitle":"Creation and the first disobedience","text":"Genesis 1–3","chapters":[[0,1],[0,2],[0,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"000","lesson":"God gives life and a good creation, and His command sets a real boundary. Distrust of His word leads to shame and broken fellowship, while His response includes both judgment and the beginning of hope.","question":"Where did the temptation challenge what God had said?","wordCount":1714,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90001,"title":"The First Baby in the World, and his Brother","subtitle":"Worship anger and responsibility","text":"Genesis 4–5","chapters":[[0,4],[0,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"001","lesson":"Cain’s anger did not make his violence inevitable. God confronted him before the murder, and the story calls us to take responsibility for resentment before it becomes harm.","question":"How did Cain respond to warning before he acted?","wordCount":624,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90002,"title":"The Great Ship That Saved Eight People","subtitle":"Judgment obedience and covenant","text":"Genesis 6–9","chapters":[[0,6],[0,7],[0,8],[0,9]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"002","lesson":"Noah’s obedience took practical form while judgment approached. The covenant after the flood also shows that God’s faithfulness is the foundation for life after rescue.","question":"What did faithfulness require before the flood came?","wordCount":2005,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90003,"title":"The Tower That Was Never Finished","subtitle":"Ambition that leaves God out","text":"Genesis 10–11","chapters":[[0,10],[0,11]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"003","lesson":"The builders sought security and a name for themselves. Their project warns against treating human unity and achievement as substitutes for dependence on God.","question":"What were the builders trying to secure for themselves?","wordCount":824,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90004,"title":"The Story of a Long Journey","subtitle":"Trusting God on the road","text":"Genesis 12","chapters":[[0,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"004","lesson":"Abram’s response involved leaving what was familiar on the strength of God’s promise. His later fear in Egypt also shows that a real beginning of faith does not make a person incapable of failure.","question":"Where did trust give way to fear in this account?","wordCount":1541,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90005,"title":"How Lot's Choice Brought Trouble and Abram's Choice Brought Blessing","subtitle":"Generosity and the danger of appearances","text":"Genesis 13–14","chapters":[[0,13],[0,14]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"005","lesson":"Abram made room for Lot to choose, while Lot judged the land by what he saw. A favourable appearance cannot tell us everything about the moral direction of a decision.","question":"What did Lot consider, and what did he overlook?","wordCount":1188,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90006,"title":"The Angel by the Well","subtitle":"God sees the person pushed aside","text":"Genesis 16","chapters":[[0,16]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"006","lesson":"Hagar’s distress was not hidden from God. This story challenges the use of family power to mistreat another person and invites attention to those whose suffering is easily ignored.","question":"Whose suffering could others in this household dismiss?","wordCount":529,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90007,"title":"The Rain of Fire That Fell on a City","subtitle":"Justice mercy and serious choices","text":"Genesis 18–19","chapters":[[0,18],[0,19]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"007","lesson":"Abraham’s intercession and the judgment on Sodom belong in the same account. God’s patience must not be confused with approval of entrenched evil.","question":"How does Abraham’s prayer reveal concern for justice?","wordCount":1579,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90008,"title":"The Boy Who Became an Archer","subtitle":"Provision beyond human rejection","text":"Genesis 21","chapters":[[0,21]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"008","lesson":"God heard the child in the wilderness when human resources had failed. His care for Hagar and Ishmael should enlarge our concern for people left without support.","question":"How did God meet Hagar in her distress?","wordCount":772,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90009,"title":"How an Angel's Voice Saved a Boy's Life","subtitle":"Trust and God’s provision","text":"Genesis 22","chapters":[[0,22]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"009","lesson":"Abraham’s trust was tested at the centre of a cherished promise, and God stopped the sacrifice of Isaac. The event points to divine provision and gives no permission to harm a child on a private religious impulse.","question":"Why must the ending at the altar govern our reading of the test?","wordCount":1167,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90010,"title":"The Story of a Journey after a Wife","subtitle":"Faithfulness in ordinary decisions","text":"Genesis 24","chapters":[[0,24]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"010","lesson":"The search for Isaac’s wife involved prayer, practical action and a response from Rebekah herself. We should seek God’s guidance with honesty and care for the people affected by our decisions.","question":"How did prayer and practical responsibility work together?","wordCount":1097,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90011,"title":"How Jacob Stole His Brother's Blessing","subtitle":"What deception does to a family","text":"Genesis 25–27","chapters":[[0,25],[0,26],[0,27]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"011","lesson":"Esau treated his birthright lightly, while Jacob and Rebekah pursued advantage through deceit. God’s purpose does not make the wrong choices of the family harmless.","question":"What damage followed the attempt to gain blessing by deception?","wordCount":1751,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90012,"title":"Jacob's Wonderful Dream","subtitle":"God meets a man far from home","text":"Genesis 28–31","chapters":[[0,28],[0,29],[0,30],[0,31]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"012","lesson":"Jacob received a promise when he was vulnerable and away from home. The later years of labour and conflict show a long process in which God’s faithfulness outlasted human manipulation.","question":"What did God promise Jacob before his circumstances improved?","wordCount":1109,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90013,"title":"A Midnight Wrestling Match","subtitle":"Humility before an uncertain meeting","text":"Genesis 32–36","chapters":[[0,32],[0,33],[0,34],[0,35],[0,36]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"013","lesson":"Jacob faced both the brother he had wronged and his own need of God. His return calls us to seek mercy rather than rely only on schemes for controlling another person’s response.","question":"What signs of humility appear before Jacob meets Esau?","wordCount":1149,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90014,"title":"The Rich Man's Son Who Was Sold as a Slave","subtitle":"Integrity when others do wrong","text":"Genesis 37; Genesis 39","chapters":[[0,37],[0,39]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"014","lesson":"Betrayal did not remove Joseph’s responsibility to resist sexual sin and serve faithfully. His obedience was costly, and the account does not promise an immediate escape from injustice.","question":"What did Joseph refuse even when refusal brought trouble?","wordCount":1239,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90015,"title":"From the Prison to the Palace","subtitle":"Service without taking God’s credit","text":"Genesis 40–41","chapters":[[0,40],[0,41]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"015","lesson":"Joseph used his ability to serve while directing attention to God. Recognition came after long waiting, rather than through a promise that faithful work must be rewarded quickly.","question":"How did Joseph speak about the source of his understanding?","wordCount":2054,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90016,"title":"How Joseph's Dream Came True","subtitle":"A guilty past comes into view","text":"Genesis 42","chapters":[[0,42]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"016","lesson":"The famine brought Joseph’s brothers into circumstances that awakened their memory of wrongdoing. A troubled conscience becomes useful when it leads to truth and repentance rather than concealment.","question":"What earlier wrong did the brothers begin to recognise?","wordCount":1550,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90017,"title":"A Lost Brother Found.","subtitle":"Changed conduct and forgiveness","text":"Genesis 43–45","chapters":[[0,43],[0,44],[0,45]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"017","lesson":"Judah’s offer to stand in Benjamin’s place contrasts with the earlier betrayal of Joseph. Forgiveness meets real wrongdoing, and a changed response can be seen in costly concern for another person.","question":"How did Judah act differently from the earlier brothers?","wordCount":1755,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90018,"title":"From the Land of Famine to the Land of Plenty","subtitle":"Providence without excusing evil","text":"Genesis 46–50","chapters":[[0,46],[0,47],[0,48],[0,49],[0,50]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"018","lesson":"Joseph distinguished his brothers’ evil intention from God’s purpose to preserve life. Trust in providence does not require calling betrayal good or refusing appropriate boundaries.","question":"What distinction does Joseph make between human intention and God’s work?","wordCount":1075,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90019,"title":"The Beautiful Baby Who Was Found in a River","subtitle":"Courage that protects vulnerable life","text":"Exodus 1–2","chapters":[[1,1],[1,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"019","lesson":"The account shows people acting courageously under oppressive power. God’s care works through practical decisions, and the lives of the vulnerable must not be treated as disposable.","question":"Who acted to preserve life when authority threatened it?","wordCount":1326,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90020,"title":"The Voice from the Burning Bush.","subtitle":"A calling larger than the servant","text":"Exodus 3–4","chapters":[[1,3],[1,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"020","lesson":"God begins with His knowledge of Israel’s suffering and answers Moses with the promise of His presence. Honest questions should lead toward obedience rather than become permanent excuses.","question":"How did God answer Moses’s sense of inadequacy?","wordCount":1515,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90021,"title":"The River That Ran Blood","subtitle":"Power that refuses correction","text":"Exodus 5–11","chapters":[[1,5],[1,6],[1,7],[1,8],[1,9],[1,10],[1,11]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"021","lesson":"Pharaoh repeatedly resisted clear demands to release the oppressed. Delay, pressure and temporary concessions did not amount to a genuine change of direction.","question":"How did Pharaoh respond when immediate pressure eased?","wordCount":1369,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90022,"title":"The Night When a Nation Was Born","subtitle":"Remembering deliverance","text":"Exodus 12–13","chapters":[[1,12],[1,13]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"022","lesson":"The Passover placed rescue within an act of obedient trust and a continuing practice of remembrance. Remembering God’s saving work should shape the life of the rescued community.","question":"What were later generations meant to learn through remembrance?","wordCount":1074,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90023,"title":"How the Sea Became Dry Land, and the Sky Rained Bread","subtitle":"Trust after rescue","text":"Exodus 14–17","chapters":[[1,14],[1,15],[1,16],[1,17]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"023","lesson":"Israel’s deliverance at the sea was followed by new tests of trust in the wilderness. Gratitude for yesterday’s rescue must become obedience in today’s need.","question":"How did the people respond when a new difficulty appeared?","wordCount":1536,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90024,"title":"The Mountain That Smoked and the Words That Were Spoken From It","subtitle":"Reverence and covenant responsibility","text":"Exodus 19–20; Exodus 24","chapters":[[1,19],[1,20],[1,24]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"024","lesson":"The encounter at Sinai joins God’s holiness with His instruction for communal life. Reverence should take practical form in worship, truthfulness and treatment of neighbours.","question":"How do the commands connect devotion to God with daily conduct?","wordCount":1133,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90025,"title":"How Aaron Made a Golden Calf, and What Became of It","subtitle":"When worship is reshaped by impatience","text":"Exodus 32–34","chapters":[[1,32],[1,33],[1,34]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"025","lesson":"The golden calf exposes the desire to make worship manageable when waiting becomes difficult. Moses’s intercession and the Lord’s response reveal both the seriousness of sin and the need for mercy.","question":"What did impatience lead the people to demand?","wordCount":1234,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90026,"title":"The Tent Where God Lived among His People","subtitle":"Willing service for a shared work","text":"Exodus 25–31; Exodus 35–40","chapters":[[1,25],[1,26],[1,27],[1,28],[1,29],[1,30],[1,31],[1,35],[1,36],[1,37],[1,38],[1,39],[1,40]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"026","lesson":"The tabernacle involved gifts, skilled work and attention to God’s instruction. Faithful service includes ordinary care in the work entrusted to us.","question":"How did willingness and careful workmanship belong together?","wordCount":1729,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90027,"title":"How They Worshipped God in the Tabernacle","subtitle":"Approaching a holy God","text":"Leviticus 1–9","chapters":[[2,1],[2,2],[2,3],[2,4],[2,5],[2,6],[2,7],[2,8],[2,9]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"027","lesson":"The sacrificial instructions teach that worship was not to be invented casually. Read them in their covenant setting and alongside the New Testament’s teaching about Christ.","question":"What does the care given to worship reveal about God’s holiness?","wordCount":1063,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90028,"title":"What Strong Drink Brought to Aaron's Sons","subtitle":"Sacred responsibility and sober judgment","text":"Leviticus 10","chapters":[[2,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"028","lesson":"Nadab and Abihu’s conduct and the instructions following it stress reverence in sacred service. Spiritual responsibility calls for discernment and disciplined conduct, rather than familiarity without care.","question":"What distinction were priests required to preserve?","wordCount":533,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90029,"title":"The Scapegoat in the Wilderness","subtitle":"Sin cannot be treated as a small matter","text":"Leviticus 16","chapters":[[2,16]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"029","lesson":"The Day of Atonement placed cleansing and reconciliation at the centre of the people’s life. Its rituals should be understood within Israel’s covenant and in relation to Christ’s completed work.","question":"What did the repeated need for cleansing teach the people?","wordCount":635,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90030,"title":"The Cluster of Grapes from the Land of Canaan","subtitle":"Fear that refuses God’s promise","text":"Numbers 13–14","chapters":[[3,13],[3,14]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"030","lesson":"The spies saw the same land but drew different conclusions about obeying God. Fear became destructive when it turned into rebellion and an attempt to retreat from the command.","question":"What made the faithful report different from the fearful one?","wordCount":1434,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90031,"title":"How the Long Journey of the Israelites Came to an End","subtitle":"Lessons the wilderness exposed","text":"Numbers 16–21","chapters":[[3,16],[3,17],[3,18],[3,19],[3,20],[3,21]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"031","lesson":"Repeated conflict in the wilderness brought pride, impatience and dependence into the open. God’s provision did not remove the people’s responsibility to trust and obey.","question":"Which response to difficulty became a repeated pattern?","wordCount":1513,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90032,"title":"What a Wise Man Learned from an Ass","subtitle":"Spiritual language and divided motives","text":"Numbers 22–25","chapters":[[3,22],[3,23],[3,24],[3,25]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"032","lesson":"Balaam’s story warns that religious speech and access to a powerful ruler do not prove a faithful heart. God’s word cannot be bent safely toward personal gain.","question":"What do Balaam’s choices reveal beyond his words?","wordCount":2366,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90033,"title":"How Moses Looked upon the Promised Land","subtitle":"Finishing with responsibility","text":"Numbers 27; Deuteronomy 31–34","chapters":[[3,27],[4,31],[4,32],[4,33],[4,34]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"033","lesson":"Moses’s final days include instruction, succession and a view of the land he would not enter. Faithful leadership prepares others to continue rather than making the work depend entirely on one person.","question":"How did Moses prepare the people for leadership after him?","wordCount":1284,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90034,"title":"The Story of Job","subtitle":"Trust when easy explanations fail","text":"Job 1–2; Job 38–42","chapters":[[17,1],[17,2],[17,38],[17,39],[17,40],[17,41],[17,42]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"034","lesson":"Job’s suffering cannot be reduced to the accusation that every sufferer must have committed a hidden wrong. The book calls for humility before God and care in what we say to people in pain.","question":"How did the friends’ certainty fail to serve Job faithfully?","wordCount":1422,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90035,"title":"The Story of a Scarlet Cord","subtitle":"Faith expressed in a risky decision","text":"Joshua 1–2","chapters":[[5,1],[5,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"035","lesson":"Rahab responded to what she had heard of the Lord and acted to protect the messengers. The account joins confession of God’s power with a practical change of allegiance.","question":"How did Rahab’s actions express the belief she described?","wordCount":970,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90036,"title":"How the River Jordan Became Dry, and the Walls of Jericho Fell Down","subtitle":"Obedience at the beginning of a new stage","text":"Joshua 3–6","chapters":[[5,3],[5,4],[5,5],[5,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"036","lesson":"Israel crossed the Jordan and faced Jericho under God’s direction. Remembering the Lord’s work was meant to teach coming generations, not simply celebrate one generation’s success.","question":"Why were memorial stones part of the crossing?","wordCount":1764,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90037,"title":"The Story of a Wedge of Gold","subtitle":"Hidden wrongdoing and communal harm","text":"Joshua 7–8","chapters":[[5,7],[5,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"037","lesson":"Achan’s concealment did not remain a private matter. The account warns against assuming that an unseen wrong carries no responsibility or effect on others.","question":"What did the hidden objects represent about Achan’s choices?","wordCount":987,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90038,"title":"How Joshua Conquered the Land of Canaan","subtitle":"Wisdom in the decisions of leadership","text":"Joshua 9–12","chapters":[[5,9],[5,10],[5,11],[5,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"038","lesson":"The Gibeonite episode exposes the danger of relying on appearances without seeking the Lord. The wider campaign belongs to Israel’s particular history and does not authorise private violence today.","question":"What inquiry did Israel’s leaders fail to make?","wordCount":1232,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90039,"title":"The Old Man Who Fought against the Giants","subtitle":"Steady faithfulness over time","text":"Joshua 14–15","chapters":[[5,14],[5,15]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"039","lesson":"Caleb’s request recalls a promise sustained through long waiting. His example encourages a consistent response to God rather than enthusiasm that disappears when circumstances change.","question":"How did Caleb connect his present request with earlier faithfulness?","wordCount":1147,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90040,"title":"The Avenger of Blood, and the Cities of Refuge","subtitle":"Justice that restrains revenge","text":"Numbers 35; Joshua 20","chapters":[[3,35],[5,20]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"040","lesson":"The cities of refuge addressed the need to distinguish cases carefully and restrain immediate vengeance. Justice requires truthful inquiry rather than unchecked retaliation.","question":"What protections prevented a rush to revenge?","wordCount":1187,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90041,"title":"The Story of an Altar beside the River","subtitle":"Listening before judging motives","text":"Joshua 22–24","chapters":[[5,22],[5,23],[5,24]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"041","lesson":"The altar dispute shows how quickly an action can be misunderstood between communities. Serious concern should lead to truthful inquiry and careful listening before condemnation.","question":"How was the apparent breach explained and resolved?","wordCount":1135,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90042,"title":"The Present That Ehud Brought to King Eglon","subtitle":"Deliverance in a troubled period","text":"Judges 3","chapters":[[6,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"042","lesson":"The judges’ stories form part of Israel’s cycle of rebellion, oppression and deliverance. Their setting must guide application, rather than turning every violent act into a command for today.","question":"How does this event fit the cycle described in Judges?","wordCount":1115,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90043,"title":"How a Woman Won a Great Victory","subtitle":"Courage in responding to God’s word","text":"Judges 4–5","chapters":[[6,4],[6,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"043","lesson":"Deborah and the events of the battle show that human expectations do not limit whom God can use. The account also calls attention to the difference between receiving an instruction and acting on it.","question":"Where did hesitation appear beside a clear instruction?","wordCount":1365,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90044,"title":"Gideon and His Brave Three Hundred","subtitle":"Dependence beyond visible strength","text":"Judges 6–8","chapters":[[6,6],[6,7],[6,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"044","lesson":"Gideon’s reduced army exposed the danger of taking credit for deliverance. His later conduct also warns that a strong beginning does not remove the need for continuing faithfulness.","question":"Why was the size of the army reduced?","wordCount":2750,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90045,"title":"Jephthah's Rash Promise, and What Came from It","subtitle":"A promise requires moral discernment","text":"Judges 10–12","chapters":[[6,10],[6,11],[6,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"045","lesson":"Jephthah’s vow brings a deeply troubling cost into the story. Scripture’s report of a vow is not blanket approval of what was promised, and devotion must not excuse harm.","question":"What makes the vow a warning rather than a simple model to copy?","wordCount":712,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90046,"title":"The Strong Man: How He Lived and How He Died","subtitle":"Giftedness without disciplined obedience","text":"Judges 13–16","chapters":[[6,13],[6,14],[6,15],[6,16]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"046","lesson":"Samson’s strength did not protect him from destructive choices. God’s use of a person’s gifts should never be confused with approval of every part of that person’s conduct.","question":"Where did Samson treat danger as something he could control?","wordCount":2823,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90047,"title":"The Idol Temple at Dan, and Its Priest","subtitle":"Religion shaped by convenience","text":"Judges 17–18","chapters":[[6,17],[6,18]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"047","lesson":"Micah’s shrine and the movement of the Danites reveal worship detached from faithfulness to God. Having religious objects and a priest did not make their choices right.","question":"How were worship and personal advantage mixed together?","wordCount":1285,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90048,"title":"How Ruth Gleaned in the Field of Boaz","subtitle":"Loyalty in ordinary acts of care","text":"Ruth 1–4","chapters":[[7,1],[7,2],[7,3],[7,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"048","lesson":"Ruth’s commitment became visible through work and care for Naomi. Boaz’s response included generosity, protection and public responsibility.","question":"How did Ruth act before she knew what the future would bring?","wordCount":1052,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90049,"title":"The Little Boy with a Linen Coat","subtitle":"Honest prayer and a listening heart","text":"1 Samuel 1–3","chapters":[[8,1],[8,2],[8,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"049","lesson":"Hannah brought real grief to God, and Samuel learned to receive a difficult word faithfully. Spiritual growth includes honesty in prayer and willingness to hear correction.","question":"How did prayer and obedience take practical form in this household?","wordCount":873,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90050,"title":"How the Idol Fell Down Before the Ark","subtitle":"God is not controlled by a sacred object","text":"1 Samuel 4–6","chapters":[[8,4],[8,5],[8,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"050","lesson":"Israel could not turn the ark into a guarantee of victory. The account exposes the danger of using religious association to avoid the issue of disobedience.","question":"What confidence did the people place in the ark?","wordCount":944,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90051,"title":"The Last of the Judges","subtitle":"Remembering help while facing change","text":"1 Samuel 7–8","chapters":[[8,7],[8,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"051","lesson":"Samuel called the people back to the Lord and marked His help. Their later request for a king raises a searching question about whose security and leadership they trusted.","question":"What did the request for a king reveal about the people’s priorities?","wordCount":901,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90052,"title":"The Tall Man Who Was Chosen King","subtitle":"Calling and outward appearance","text":"1 Samuel 9–10","chapters":[[8,9],[8,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"052","lesson":"Saul’s selection warns against reducing fitness for leadership to what impresses the eye. Receiving an opportunity to serve also brings responsibility before God.","question":"What should be weighed beyond a leader’s appearance?","wordCount":1606,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90053,"title":"How Saul Saved the Eyes of the Men of Jabesh","subtitle":"Leadership that serves threatened people","text":"1 Samuel 11–12","chapters":[[8,11],[8,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"053","lesson":"Saul’s early response brought practical help to Jabesh. Samuel also reminded the nation that deliverance did not remove their obligation to fear the Lord and serve faithfully.","question":"What duty remained after the immediate victory?","wordCount":911,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90054,"title":"The Brave Young Prince","subtitle":"Courage and the burden of rash commands","text":"1 Samuel 13–14","chapters":[[8,13],[8,14]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"054","lesson":"Jonathan’s trust stands beside Saul’s impatient decisions and harmful oath. Authority should be used with care for the people who must live under its instructions.","question":"How did a leader’s command create difficulty for others?","wordCount":1214,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90055,"title":"Saul's Great Sin and His Great Loss","subtitle":"Obedience cannot be replaced by an offering","text":"1 Samuel 15","chapters":[[8,15]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"055","lesson":"Saul’s religious explanation did not change his refusal to obey. Confession must move beyond protecting appearances into acceptance of correction.","question":"How did Saul describe what he had kept?","wordCount":823,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90056,"title":"The Shepherd Boy of Bethlehem","subtitle":"The heart behind the appearance","text":"1 Samuel 16","chapters":[[8,16]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"056","lesson":"David’s anointing directs attention beyond outward impressiveness. The Lord’s knowledge of the heart challenges shallow measures of spiritual readiness.","question":"What did Samuel need to learn about judging appearances?","wordCount":1333,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90057,"title":"The Shepherd Boy's Fight with the Giant","subtitle":"Courage that honours the Lord","text":"1 Samuel 17","chapters":[[8,17]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"057","lesson":"David joined confidence in God with practical means he knew how to use. The story centres on the Lord’s honour rather than a formula for fulfilling every personal ambition.","question":"Whose honour did David defend in his answer to Goliath?","wordCount":1195,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90058,"title":"The Little Boy Looking for the Arrows","subtitle":"Friendship that accepts a personal cost","text":"1 Samuel 18–20","chapters":[[8,18],[8,19],[8,20]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"058","lesson":"Jonathan’s loyalty to David brought him into tension with Saul. Faithful friendship seeks another person’s good without making personal advantage its condition.","question":"What did Jonathan risk in protecting David?","wordCount":1689,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90059,"title":"Where David Found the Giant's Sword","subtitle":"Fear and the danger of concealment","text":"1 Samuel 21–22","chapters":[[8,21],[8,22]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"059","lesson":"David’s flight shows the pressure of danger and the far-reaching consequences of words spoken under fear. Trust requires honesty and concern for those affected by our choices.","question":"Who suffered consequences beyond David himself?","wordCount":1240,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90060,"title":"How David Spared Saul's Life","subtitle":"Restraint when revenge is possible","text":"1 Samuel 23–27","chapters":[[8,23],[8,24],[8,25],[8,26],[8,27]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"060","lesson":"David’s opportunities to kill Saul tested his use of power. Refusing revenge can be an act of strength while appropriate distance and protection remain necessary.","question":"What did David refuse to do when he had the advantage?","wordCount":1145,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90061,"title":"The Last Days of King Saul","subtitle":"The direction of a long refusal","text":"1 Samuel 28–31","chapters":[[8,28],[8,29],[8,30],[8,31]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"061","lesson":"Saul’s final days bring fear, loss and earlier disobedience together. A spiritual crisis should not lead us to practices that God has forbidden.","question":"Where did Saul turn when he was afraid?","wordCount":1588,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90062,"title":"The Shepherd Boy Becomes a King","subtitle":"Receiving responsibility without forgetting grief","text":"2 Samuel 1–5","chapters":[[9,1],[9,2],[9,3],[9,4],[9,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"062","lesson":"David’s rise to kingship unfolds through loss, division and reconciliation. The account calls for patient responsibility rather than delight in every rival’s downfall.","question":"How did David respond to news about Saul and Jonathan?","wordCount":991,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90063,"title":"The Sound in the Tree-Tops","subtitle":"Seeking God beyond yesterday’s answer","text":"2 Samuel 5–8","chapters":[[9,5],[9,6],[9,7],[9,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"063","lesson":"David sought the Lord in successive confrontations and received differing directions. Past success is not a reason to stop listening or assume that our own method must always work.","question":"Why did David inquire again rather than repeat his earlier action?","wordCount":1325,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90064,"title":"The Cripple at the King's Table","subtitle":"Covenant kindness toward the vulnerable","text":"2 Samuel 9","chapters":[[9,9]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"064","lesson":"David’s care for Mephibosheth honoured his commitment to Jonathan. Mercy can become tangible through a secure place, restored provision and faithful treatment.","question":"What did David’s promise lead him to provide?","wordCount":806,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90065,"title":"The Prophet's Story of the Little Lamb","subtitle":"Truthful repentance and costly mercy","text":"2 Samuel 11–12","chapters":[[9,11],[9,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"065","lesson":"Nathan confronted David’s misuse of royal power and his attempt to hide sin. God’s forgiveness did not call the wrongdoing good or erase every consequence.","question":"How did the parable expose what David had tried to conceal?","wordCount":1384,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90066,"title":"David's Handsome Son, and How He Stole The Kingdom","subtitle":"A household wounded by unaddressed wrong","text":"2 Samuel 13–15","chapters":[[9,13],[9,14],[9,15]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"066","lesson":"The violence and ambitions in David’s family show the spread of harm when power is abused and justice is mishandled. Appearance and popularity do not establish righteous leadership.","question":"What unresolved wrong helped prepare the later crisis?","wordCount":2523,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90067,"title":"Absalom in the Wood:  David on the Throne","subtitle":"Grief and the responsibilities of rule","text":"2 Samuel 16–20","chapters":[[9,16],[9,17],[9,18],[9,19],[9,20]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"067","lesson":"David’s grief for Absalom was real, while his public responsibilities remained. Leaders must receive truthful counsel even when personal sorrow makes it difficult.","question":"What did David need to hear after the battle?","wordCount":2361,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90068,"title":"The Angel with the Drawn Sword on Mount Moriah","subtitle":"Responsibility without shifting the cost","text":"2 Samuel 24; 1 Chronicles 21","chapters":[[9,24],[12,21]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"068","lesson":"David acknowledged his fault and refused an offering that cost him nothing. The account directs attention to honest responsibility rather than transferring the burden to someone else.","question":"How did David respond when he could have received the offering materials freely?","wordCount":1045,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90069,"title":"Solomon on David's Throne","subtitle":"Succession and faithful responsibility","text":"1 Kings 1–2","chapters":[[10,1],[10,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"069","lesson":"The transition from David to Solomon involved competing claims and solemn counsel. Receiving authority must be joined to a readiness to walk in God’s ways.","question":"What did David emphasise in his charge to Solomon?","wordCount":793,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90070,"title":"The Wise Young King","subtitle":"Wisdom for serving others","text":"1 Kings 3–4","chapters":[[10,3],[10,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"070","lesson":"Solomon’s request recognised his need to govern well. Wisdom should be sought for truthful judgment and the good of those entrusted to our care.","question":"What purpose shaped Solomon’s request?","wordCount":832,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90071,"title":"The House of God on Mount Moriah","subtitle":"The temple and the God beyond it","text":"1 Kings 5–8","chapters":[[10,5],[10,6],[10,7],[10,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"071","lesson":"The dedication prayer recognises that no building can contain God. A place of worship should lead to humble dependence, prayer and faithful living.","question":"How did Solomon speak about God in relation to the temple?","wordCount":940,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90072,"title":"The Last Days of Solomon's Reign","subtitle":"A divided heart after great privilege","text":"1 Kings 9–11","chapters":[[10,9],[10,10],[10,11]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"072","lesson":"Solomon’s later course shows that wisdom, wealth and earlier devotion do not make compromise harmless. Continuing obedience matters after a strong beginning.","question":"What gradually divided Solomon’s devotion?","wordCount":1166,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90073,"title":"The Breaking Up of a Great Kingdom","subtitle":"Listening when authority is tested","text":"1 Kings 12","chapters":[[10,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"073","lesson":"Rehoboam’s rejection of wise counsel deepened division. Leadership that treats people’s burdens with contempt undermines the trust it needs to serve.","question":"Which counsel did Rehoboam choose, and what followed?","wordCount":1041,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90074,"title":"The King Who Led Israel to Sin, and the Prophet Who Was Slain by a Lion","subtitle":"Worship used to secure political control","text":"1 Kings 12–14","chapters":[[10,12],[10,13],[10,14]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"074","lesson":"Jeroboam reshaped worship to protect his rule. Spiritual practice becomes corrupt when truth is made subordinate to convenience or a leader’s need for control.","question":"What fear influenced Jeroboam’s religious policy?","wordCount":1551,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90075,"title":"The Prophet Whose Prayer Raised a Boy to Life","subtitle":"Trust and provision in scarcity","text":"1 Kings 17","chapters":[[10,17]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"075","lesson":"Elijah and the widow’s household depended on the Lord in a time of severe need. The account directs attention to God’s faithfulness without giving a formula for exploiting a vulnerable person’s resources.","question":"How did God’s word sustain the household?","wordCount":1314,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90076,"title":"The Prayer That Was Answered in Fire","subtitle":"A divided allegiance confronted","text":"1 Kings 18","chapters":[[10,18]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"076","lesson":"The confrontation on Carmel challenged an attempt to keep incompatible loyalties together. The Lord’s answer called the people toward a clear response rather than religious indecision.","question":"What decision did Elijah put before the people?","wordCount":1478,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90077,"title":"The Voice That Spoke to Elijah in the Mount","subtitle":"God’s care for a weary servant","text":"1 Kings 19","chapters":[[10,19]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"077","lesson":"Elijah’s exhaustion was met with practical care and renewed instruction. A servant’s discouragement should be handled with patience rather than easy condemnation.","question":"What forms of care came before Elijah’s renewed commission?","wordCount":1303,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90078,"title":"The Wounded Prophet and His Story","subtitle":"Responsibility after an unexpected victory","text":"1 Kings 20","chapters":[[10,20]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"078","lesson":"Ahab’s success did not give him authority to disregard the Lord’s word. Receiving help and acting faithfully afterward are related but distinct responsibilities.","question":"What choice was challenged after the victory?","wordCount":610,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90079,"title":"What Ahab Paid for His Vineyard","subtitle":"Coveting strengthened by abused power","text":"1 Kings 21","chapters":[[10,21]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"079","lesson":"Naboth’s vineyard became the target of desire backed by dishonest authority. The account exposes the injustice of using influence to take what belongs to another.","question":"How did private desire become public wrongdoing?","wordCount":687,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90080,"title":"The Arrow That Killed a King","subtitle":"Truth when flattery is welcome","text":"1 Kings 22","chapters":[[10,22]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"080","lesson":"Micaiah’s message opposed what the king wanted to hear. Spiritual counsel must be weighed by truth rather than its ability to reassure a person in power.","question":"How did the king treat an unwelcome warning?","wordCount":1048,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90081,"title":"Elijah's Chariot of Fire","subtitle":"A continuing work beyond one servant","text":"2 Kings 1–2","chapters":[[11,1],[11,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"081","lesson":"Elijah’s departure did not end the Lord’s work. Elisha’s calling encourages faithfulness across a change of leadership and directs honour toward God rather than a human personality.","question":"What continued after Elijah was taken away?","wordCount":756,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90082,"title":"A Spring Sweetened by Salt; and Water That Looked Like Blood","subtitle":"Learning to depend on the Lord","text":"2 Kings 2–3","chapters":[[11,2],[11,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"082","lesson":"The early accounts of Elisha’s ministry show need being brought before God. Their particular signs should not be turned into techniques detached from His word and purpose.","question":"What need does this event bring into the open?","wordCount":1009,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90083,"title":"The Pot of Oil and the Pot of Poison","subtitle":"Provision that reaches ordinary households","text":"2 Kings 4","chapters":[[11,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"083","lesson":"The widow’s need and the community’s hunger mattered in the prophet’s ministry. Spiritual concern should include attention to debt, food and the practical burdens people carry.","question":"Which everyday needs were taken seriously?","wordCount":838,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90084,"title":"The Little Boy at Shunem","subtitle":"Hospitality grief and persistent appeal","text":"2 Kings 4","chapters":[[11,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"084","lesson":"The Shunammite woman’s hospitality was followed by a gift she could not control and grief she brought urgently to the prophet. The story honours the Lord’s power while treating her distress as real.","question":"How did she respond when the child died?","wordCount":896,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90085,"title":"How a Little Girl Helped to Cure a Leper","subtitle":"Humility beyond rank and expectation","text":"2 Kings 5","chapters":[[11,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"085","lesson":"Naaman nearly rejected help because it came in a form he considered beneath him. The captive girl and the servants offered counsel his status could not replace.","question":"What expectations almost kept Naaman from responding?","wordCount":1420,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90086,"title":"The Chariots of Fire around Elisha","subtitle":"Mercy when an enemy is in your power","text":"2 Kings 6","chapters":[[11,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"086","lesson":"Elisha directed the king toward feeding the captured force rather than killing them. An opportunity to dominate another person can become an opportunity for restraint and mercy.","question":"What response did Elisha require toward the captured men?","wordCount":645,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90087,"title":"What the Lepers Found in the Camp","subtitle":"Good news that should not be withheld","text":"2 Kings 6–7","chapters":[[11,6],[11,7]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"087","lesson":"The men who discovered provision outside the city recognised that keeping it to themselves was wrong. A gift received can create responsibility toward people still in need.","question":"When did private discovery become a duty to tell others?","wordCount":947,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90088,"title":"Jehu, the Furious Driver of His Chariot","subtitle":"Zeal and the need for a faithful heart","text":"2 Kings 8–10","chapters":[[11,8],[11,9],[11,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"088","lesson":"Jehu’s forceful actions did not establish that every part of his life was faithful. Visible zeal should not become a substitute for examining continuing obedience.","question":"What does the account require us to consider beyond Jehu’s energy?","wordCount":2004,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90089,"title":"Elisha and the Bow; Jonah and Nineveh","subtitle":"God’s work and the limits of our compassion","text":"2 Kings 13; Jonah 1–4","chapters":[[11,13],[31,1],[31,2],[31,3],[31,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"089","lesson":"The closing scenes of Elisha’s life and Jonah’s mission point beyond the strength or preferences of a servant. Jonah especially exposes resentment when God shows mercy to people the prophet dislikes.","question":"Why did Jonah struggle with Nineveh’s repentance?","wordCount":1736,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90090,"title":"How the Ten Tribes Were Lost","subtitle":"The accumulated cost of turning away","text":"2 Kings 15–17","chapters":[[11,15],[11,16],[11,17]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"090","lesson":"The fall of the northern kingdom is explained in relation to persistent covenant unfaithfulness. The narrative invites sober attention to repeated choices rather than confidence that earlier privilege guarantees safety.","question":"What pattern does the account identify behind the collapse?","wordCount":705,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90091,"title":"The First Four Kings of Judah.","subtitle":"Seeking God beyond appearances of stability","text":"1 Kings 14–15; 2 Chronicles 11–20","chapters":[[10,14],[10,15],[13,11],[13,12],[13,13],[13,14],[13,15],[13,16],[13,17],[13,18],[13,19],[13,20]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"091","lesson":"Judah’s early kings show differing responses to worship, counsel and conflict. A secure throne is not a reliable measure of faithfulness before God.","question":"What distinguishes reform from merely preserving power?","wordCount":1517,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90092,"title":"The Little Boy Who Was Crowned King","subtitle":"Faithfulness beyond the influence of a mentor","text":"2 Kings 11–12; 2 Chronicles 22–24","chapters":[[11,11],[11,12],[13,22],[13,23],[13,24]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"092","lesson":"Joash’s story shows the value of wise guidance and the danger of a later change in direction. A borrowed religious commitment must become a personally sustained obedience.","question":"What changed when Joash no longer had the same guiding influence?","wordCount":1347,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90093,"title":"Three Kings and a Great Prophet","subtitle":"A holy God and a servant’s response","text":"2 Kings 14–16; Isaiah 6","chapters":[[11,14],[11,15],[11,16],[22,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"093","lesson":"The kings’ differing courses stand beside Isaiah’s encounter with divine holiness. The proper response includes confession, cleansing and willingness to serve.","question":"What did Isaiah recognise before he offered himself for service?","wordCount":1950,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90094,"title":"The Good King Hezekiah","subtitle":"Trust during public danger","text":"2 Kings 18–20; Isaiah 36–39","chapters":[[11,18],[11,19],[11,20],[22,36],[22,37],[22,38],[22,39]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"094","lesson":"Hezekiah brought the threatening message before the Lord. His later mistakes also warn that deliverance and answered prayer do not remove the need for humility.","question":"What did Hezekiah do with the enemy’s message?","wordCount":1759,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90095,"title":"The Lost Book Found in the Temple","subtitle":"Hearing Scripture as a call to change","text":"2 Kings 22–23","chapters":[[11,22],[11,23]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"095","lesson":"Josiah responded to the recovered book with grief and action. Discovering what Scripture says is not the end of obedience, but its beginning.","question":"How did Josiah respond beyond simply listening?","wordCount":1423,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90096,"title":"The Last Four Kings of Judah, and The Weeping Prophet","subtitle":"A warning rejected over time","text":"2 Kings 24–25; Jeremiah 36–39","chapters":[[11,24],[11,25],[23,36],[23,37],[23,38],[23,39]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"096","lesson":"Jeremiah’s costly witness continued while the kingdom moved toward disaster. The truth of a warning is not measured by whether its messenger is popular or protected from suffering.","question":"How were unwelcome words treated before the city fell?","wordCount":1986,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90097,"title":"What Ezekiel Saw in the Valley","subtitle":"Hope grounded in God’s power","text":"Ezekiel 37","chapters":[[25,37]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"097","lesson":"The vision of the dry bones addresses a people who considered their hope lost. Its explanation concerns God’s restoring work and must be read with the interpretation given in the chapter.","question":"How does the chapter itself explain the bones?","wordCount":1111,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90098,"title":"The Jewish Captives in the Court of the King","subtitle":"Faithfulness away from home","text":"Daniel 1–2","chapters":[[26,1],[26,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"098","lesson":"Daniel and his companions faced pressure to adapt at the royal court. Their conduct joins practical wisdom with a settled refusal to abandon devotion to God.","question":"How did they respond firmly without unnecessary hostility?","wordCount":1605,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90099,"title":"The Golden Image and the Fiery Furnace","subtitle":"Faithfulness without bargaining over the outcome","text":"Daniel 3","chapters":[[26,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"099","lesson":"The three men refused to make obedience conditional on being rescued. Their trust in God included readiness to remain faithful when the cost was uncertain.","question":"What did their answer say about the possibility of not being delivered?","wordCount":962,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90100,"title":"The Tree That Was Cut Down and Grew Again","subtitle":"Pride brought under divine rule","text":"Daniel 4","chapters":[[26,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"100","lesson":"Nebuchadnezzar’s experience challenged his claim to be the source of his own greatness. Authority and achievement should lead to humility rather than self-exaltation.","question":"What did the king fail to acknowledge before his humiliation?","wordCount":1063,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90101,"title":"The Writing upon the Wall","subtitle":"Accountability after a known warning","text":"Daniel 5","chapters":[[26,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"101","lesson":"Belshazzar’s privilege did not erase what he should have learned from the past. Knowledge brings responsibility, especially when power encourages careless contempt.","question":"What did Belshazzar know but fail to take seriously?","wordCount":1044,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90102,"title":"Daniel in the Den of Lions.","subtitle":"A prayer habit that survives pressure","text":"Daniel 6","chapters":[[26,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"102","lesson":"Daniel’s integrity at work and steady prayer belonged together. Faithfulness was established before the crisis and was not a performance begun for the occasion.","question":"What did Daniel continue doing after the decree?","wordCount":1151,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90103,"title":"The Story of a Joyous Journey","subtitle":"Returning with grateful hope","text":"Ezra 1–2; Psalms 126","chapters":[[14,1],[14,2],[18,126]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"103","lesson":"The return from exile depended on the Lord’s faithfulness across a long period of loss. Gratitude should become practical commitment to the work of restoration.","question":"What did the returning people have to begin rebuilding?","wordCount":1413,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90104,"title":"The New Temple on Mount Moriah","subtitle":"Perseverance in a shared work","text":"Ezra 3–6; Haggai 1–2","chapters":[[14,3],[14,4],[14,5],[14,6],[36,1],[36,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"104","lesson":"Rebuilding brought joy, opposition and discouragement. The people needed renewed attention to God’s word as well as practical perseverance.","question":"What interrupted the work, and what renewed it?","wordCount":1336,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90105,"title":"The Beautiful Queen of Persia","subtitle":"Using influence to protect others","text":"Esther 1–10","chapters":[[16,1],[16,2],[16,3],[16,4],[16,5],[16,6],[16,7],[16,8],[16,9],[16,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"105","lesson":"Esther’s position became a responsibility toward threatened people. She sought support and acted carefully rather than treating courage as the absence of danger.","question":"How did Esther prepare before speaking to the king?","wordCount":3158,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90106,"title":"The Scribe Who Wrote the Old Testament","subtitle":"Learning in order to practise and teach","text":"Ezra 7–10","chapters":[[14,7],[14,8],[14,9],[14,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"106","lesson":"Ezra’s commitment joined study with obedience and instruction. Spiritual knowledge should reach conduct before it is used to direct others.","question":"How are learning, doing and teaching connected in Ezra’s example?","wordCount":1368,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90107,"title":"The Nobleman Who Built the Wall of Jerusalem","subtitle":"Prayer that leads to responsible action","text":"Nehemiah 1–6","chapters":[[15,1],[15,2],[15,3],[15,4],[15,5],[15,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"107","lesson":"Nehemiah brought the city’s condition before God and then planned and worked. Prayer did not remove the need for practical preparation, cooperation and vigilance.","question":"How did prayer shape the action Nehemiah took?","wordCount":1734,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90108,"title":"Ezra's Great Bible Class in Jerusalem","subtitle":"Understanding Scripture as a community","text":"Nehemiah 8–13","chapters":[[15,8],[15,9],[15,10],[15,11],[15,12],[15,13]],"kind":"story","category":"Old Testament","file":"108","lesson":"The public reading included explanation so that the people could understand. A faithful response involved worship, repentance and renewed duties in daily life.","question":"What helped the listeners move from hearing words to understanding them?","wordCount":986,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90109,"title":"The Angel by the Altar","subtitle":"A promise meets human doubt","text":"Luke 1","chapters":[[41,1]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"109","lesson":"Zechariah and Elizabeth’s story places long waiting beside God’s purpose. The account invites trust while showing that a faithful servant can still need correction.","question":"How did Zechariah respond to the promised answer?","wordCount":1423,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90110,"title":"The Manger of Bethlehem","subtitle":"God’s promise in humble circumstances","text":"Luke 1–2","chapters":[[41,1],[41,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"110","lesson":"The birth of Jesus directs attention to God’s faithfulness rather than outward status. Worship responds to the One announced, not to the comfort of the setting.","question":"Who first heard the announcement, and how did they respond?","wordCount":1174,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90111,"title":"The Star and the Wise Men","subtitle":"Seeking Christ amid opposed responses","text":"Matthew 2","chapters":[[39,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"111","lesson":"The visitors’ worship contrasts with Herod’s violent fear of losing power. The coming of Christ exposes what different hearts want to preserve or surrender.","question":"How did the visitors and Herod respond differently to the same news?","wordCount":1129,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90112,"title":"The Boy in His Father's House","subtitle":"Growing in the Father’s purpose","text":"Luke 2","chapters":[[41,2]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"112","lesson":"The account of Jesus in the temple highlights His relationship to the Father and His growth within family life. Its details should guide our understanding rather than imagined scenes from His childhood.","question":"What did Jesus’s answer reveal to His parents?","wordCount":875,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90113,"title":"The Prophet in the Wilderness","subtitle":"Repentance that bears fruit","text":"Matthew 3; Luke 3","chapters":[[39,3],[41,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"113","lesson":"John’s ministry called people beyond religious identity into a changed response to God. Genuine repentance must reach the conduct appropriate to each person’s responsibilities.","question":"What practical fruit did John require of those who came?","wordCount":845,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90114,"title":"Jesus in the Desert, and beside the River","subtitle":"Faithfulness under temptation","text":"Matthew 4; John 1","chapters":[[39,4],[42,1]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"114","lesson":"Jesus answered temptation in obedience to the Father and with Scripture rightly applied. A spiritual-sounding quotation must not be detached from God’s whole instruction.","question":"How was Scripture used and misused in the temptation account?","wordCount":1429,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90115,"title":"The Water Jars at the Wedding Feast","subtitle":"A sign that points beyond itself","text":"John 2–3","chapters":[[42,2],[42,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"115","lesson":"The wedding sign directs attention to Jesus’s glory and the disciples’ belief. Gifts from Him must not be separated from the question of who He is.","question":"What response did the sign produce in the disciples?","wordCount":1126,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90116,"title":"The Stranger at the Well","subtitle":"Grace and truth in one encounter","text":"John 4","chapters":[[42,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"116","lesson":"Jesus crossed a social boundary while speaking truthfully about the woman’s life. Her testimony led others toward hearing Him themselves.","question":"How did the townspeople move beyond relying only on her report?","wordCount":1382,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90117,"title":"The Story of a Boy in Capernaum, and of a Riot in Nazareth","subtitle":"Receiving a word that challenges familiarity","text":"John 4; Luke 4","chapters":[[42,4],[41,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"117","lesson":"The royal official’s appeal and the rejection at Nazareth reveal differing responses to Jesus. Familiarity with a place or reputation is not the same as faith.","question":"What made Jesus’s word difficult for the listeners at Nazareth?","wordCount":1137,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90118,"title":"A Net Full of Fishes","subtitle":"A call that reaches ordinary work","text":"Luke 5","chapters":[[41,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"118","lesson":"Peter’s response to the catch moved from success at fishing to recognition of his need before Jesus. The call changed the direction of his life rather than simply improving his income.","question":"How did Peter respond when he recognised Jesus’s authority?","wordCount":822,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90119,"title":"The Leper, and the Man Let Down through the Roof","subtitle":"Compassion and authority to forgive","text":"Mark 1–2; Luke 5","chapters":[[40,1],[40,2],[41,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"119","lesson":"Jesus took physical suffering seriously while addressing the deeper question of forgiveness. The accounts call us to notice both His mercy and His identity.","question":"What question about Jesus arose around the paralysed man?","wordCount":1056,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90120,"title":"The Cripple at the Pool, and the Withered Hand in the Synagogue","subtitle":"Mercy challenged by hardened judgment","text":"John 5; Mark 3","chapters":[[42,5],[40,3]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"120","lesson":"The healings exposed a dispute about doing good and recognising Jesus’s authority. Religious argument must not become a reason to lose concern for a suffering person.","question":"How did the observers respond to the person who was helped?","wordCount":1157,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90121,"title":"The Twelve Disciples and the Sermon on the Mount","subtitle":"Hearing that becomes obedience","text":"Matthew 5–7; Luke 6","chapters":[[39,5],[39,6],[39,7],[41,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"121","lesson":"Jesus’s teaching addresses motives as well as outward acts. The ending of the sermon distinguishes hearing His words from building life on them.","question":"What does the contrast between the two builders require of a listener?","wordCount":1631,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90122,"title":"The Captain's Servant, the Widow's Son, and the Woman Who Was a Sinner","subtitle":"Humility mercy and a response of love","text":"Luke 7","chapters":[[41,7]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"122","lesson":"The accounts bring authority, grief and forgiveness into view. Love expressed toward Jesus should be read alongside a truthful recognition of need and received mercy.","question":"How does the dinner conversation expose differing responses to forgiveness?","wordCount":1371,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90123,"title":"Some Stories That Jesus Told By The Sea","subtitle":"A parable asks the listener to respond","text":"Matthew 13; Mark 4","chapters":[[39,13],[40,4]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"123","lesson":"The kingdom parables invite careful hearing rather than surface familiarity. Jesus’s explanations should govern interpretation, especially where the details may be misunderstood.","question":"What response does the explanation of the sower ask from the hearer?","wordCount":1217,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90124,"title":"\"Peace, Be Still\"","subtitle":"Who is present in the storm","text":"Mark 4–5","chapters":[[40,4],[40,5]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"124","lesson":"The disciples’ fear included a mistaken conclusion about Jesus’s care. The calm directed them toward His identity and authority rather than merely a safer journey.","question":"Why does the storm account end with a question about Jesus?","wordCount":776,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90125,"title":"The Little Girl Who Was Raised to Life","subtitle":"Trust amid delay and grief","text":"Mark 5; Luke 8","chapters":[[40,5],[41,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"125","lesson":"The intertwined accounts show that a delay in one person’s appeal did not place the situation beyond Jesus’s authority. Faith looks to Him rather than to control of the timetable.","question":"How did the interruption affect the circumstances Jairus faced?","wordCount":1042,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90126,"title":"A Dancing Girl, and What Was Given Her","subtitle":"A guilty conscience under social pressure","text":"Mark 6; Matthew 14","chapters":[[40,6],[39,14]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"126","lesson":"Herod’s concern for appearances and his oath helped carry him into a terrible act. A rash promise and the fear of embarrassment must not be allowed to excuse harm.","question":"What pressures influenced Herod’s decision?","wordCount":976,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90127,"title":"The Feast Beside the Sea, and What Followed It","subtitle":"Receiving provision and hearing the giver","text":"John 6; Mark 6","chapters":[[42,6],[40,6]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"127","lesson":"The feeding sign is followed by a searching conversation about Jesus Himself. Seeking His gifts without receiving His word leaves the central question unanswered.","question":"What did people seek after the meal, and what did Jesus teach?","wordCount":1335,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90128,"title":"The Answer to a Mother's Prayer","subtitle":"Persistent appeal and humble trust","text":"Matthew 15; Mark 7","chapters":[[39,15],[40,7]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"128","lesson":"The woman brought her daughter’s need to Jesus and continued appealing to Him. Read the exchange in its setting rather than using it to dismiss people who seek help.","question":"What does her answer reveal about the trust she placed in Him?","wordCount":1183,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90129,"title":"The Glory of Jesus on the Mountain","subtitle":"Listening to the Son","text":"Matthew 16–17; Luke 9","chapters":[[39,16],[39,17],[41,9]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"129","lesson":"The transfiguration directs the disciples toward Jesus and His coming suffering. A striking experience should deepen attention to His word rather than replace it.","question":"What command accompanied the revelation on the mountain?","wordCount":1451,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90130,"title":"The Little Child in the Arms of Jesus","subtitle":"Greatness that serves rather than competes","text":"Mark 9–10; Matthew 18","chapters":[[40,9],[40,10],[39,18]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"130","lesson":"Jesus answered the disciples’ concern for status by directing them toward humble service. Receiving the small and vulnerable is part of how love is made visible.","question":"How did Jesus challenge the disciples’ measure of greatness?","wordCount":829,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90131,"title":"At the Feast of Tabernacles","subtitle":"Judging Jesus by truth rather than appearance","text":"John 7–8","chapters":[[42,7],[42,8]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"131","lesson":"Public disagreement about Jesus exposed mixed motives and assumptions. His teaching calls for honest judgment and response to the truth He reveals.","question":"What assumptions prevented some listeners from considering His words fairly?","wordCount":862,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90132,"title":"The Man with Clay on His Face","subtitle":"A received gift meets resistance","text":"John 9","chapters":[[42,9]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"132","lesson":"The healed man’s testimony became clearer under questioning while some observers resisted the evidence. Confidence in religious status must not close the heart to truth.","question":"How did the man’s understanding develop as others challenged him?","wordCount":1070,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90133,"title":"The Good Shepherd and the Good Samaritan","subtitle":"Care that knows and serves people","text":"John 10; Luke 10","chapters":[[42,10],[41,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"133","lesson":"The shepherd teaching and the Samaritan parable direct attention to protective care and practical mercy. Love is shown through responsibility rather than distance from another person’s need.","question":"What concrete actions made the Samaritan a neighbour?","wordCount":1180,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90134,"title":"Lazarus Raised to Life","subtitle":"Compassion beside the promise of life","text":"John 11","chapters":[[42,11]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"134","lesson":"Jesus’s tears and His call at the tomb belong together. The sign points to His authority over death without promising every bereaved family an immediate reversal of loss.","question":"What do Jesus’s grief and His authority reveal together?","wordCount":1012,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90135,"title":"Some Parables in Perea","subtitle":"The heart exposed through Jesus’s stories","text":"Luke 11–15","chapters":[[41,11],[41,12],[41,13],[41,14],[41,15]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"135","lesson":"These parables address prayer, pride, mercy and repentance. Each should be read with the people and question that prompted it, rather than forced into an isolated moral slogan.","question":"Who was listening, and what response was Jesus seeking?","wordCount":1678,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90136,"title":"The Poor Rich Man, and the Rich Poor Man","subtitle":"Wealth cannot settle a person’s standing before God","text":"Luke 16; Luke 18","chapters":[[41,16],[41,18]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"136","lesson":"The accounts challenge complacency built on possessions and outward success. The treatment of a neighbour and the response to God’s word reveal what wealth cannot provide.","question":"What did the rich man fail to take seriously during his life?","wordCount":1510,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90137,"title":"Jesus at Jericho","subtitle":"An encounter that reaches everyday conduct","text":"Luke 18–19; Mark 10","chapters":[[41,18],[41,19],[40,10]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"137","lesson":"Jesus’s mercy met both public need and the life of a despised tax collector. Zacchaeus’s response reached his money and the wrongs done to others.","question":"How did Zacchaeus connect his response with restitution?","wordCount":1315,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90138,"title":"Palm Sunday","subtitle":"Receiving a king whose way challenges expectation","text":"Luke 19; John 12","chapters":[[41,19],[42,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"138","lesson":"The entry into Jerusalem invited honour to Jesus while moving toward His suffering. Worship should receive His purpose rather than use Him to endorse our own.","question":"How does the approaching suffering shape the meaning of the welcome?","wordCount":954,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90139,"title":"The Last Visits of Jesus to the Temple","subtitle":"Religious appearance brought under scrutiny","text":"Matthew 21–23; Mark 11–12","chapters":[[39,21],[39,22],[39,23],[40,11],[40,12]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"139","lesson":"Jesus’s final public teaching exposed hypocrisy and challenged His hearers’ response to God. Visible religious activity must be examined alongside justice, mercy and faithfulness.","question":"What conduct did Jesus expose beneath public appearances?","wordCount":1640,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90140,"title":"The Parables on the Mount of Olives","subtitle":"Watchfulness expressed in faithful responsibility","text":"Matthew 24–25; Mark 13","chapters":[[39,24],[39,25],[40,13]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"140","lesson":"Jesus’s teaching calls His followers to readiness and faithful service. Curiosity about timing should not displace the responsibilities He makes clear.","question":"How is readiness shown in the conduct of the servants?","wordCount":1049,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90141,"title":"The Last Supper","subtitle":"Love that serves and remembers","text":"Luke 22; John 13–17","chapters":[[41,22],[42,13],[42,14],[42,15],[42,16],[42,17]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"141","lesson":"The meal, the washing of feet and the farewell teaching bring Jesus’s love and coming sacrifice into view. Remembering Him should shape humble service and obedience.","question":"How did Jesus demonstrate the love He commanded?","wordCount":1491,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90142,"title":"The Olive Orchard and the High Priest's Hall","subtitle":"Faithfulness when pressure exposes the heart","text":"Matthew 26; Luke 22","chapters":[[39,26],[41,22]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"142","lesson":"Jesus prayed in submission to the Father while the disciples struggled and Peter later denied Him. Confidence in our own strength must give way to watchful dependence.","question":"How did Peter’s response differ from his earlier promise?","wordCount":1445,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90143,"title":"The Crown of Thorns","subtitle":"Truth before threatened power","text":"Matthew 27; Luke 23; John 18–19","chapters":[[39,27],[41,23],[42,18],[42,19]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"143","lesson":"The trials reveal injustice, mockery and the pressure of public opinion. The Christian response to Christ’s suffering should include truthful judgment and refusal to join cruelty.","question":"How did concern for position or approval affect the decisions made?","wordCount":1623,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90144,"title":"The Darkest Day of All the World","subtitle":"The cross and the seriousness of saving love","text":"Luke 23; John 19","chapters":[[41,23],[42,19]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"144","lesson":"Jesus’s death must be read in the light of the Gospel’s witness to His identity and mission. The cross calls for repentance, faith and love rather than distant sympathy alone.","question":"What do Jesus’s words and the witnesses’ responses reveal?","wordCount":1544,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90145,"title":"The Brightest Day of All the World","subtitle":"Resurrection hope grounded in Christ","text":"Luke 24; John 20","chapters":[[41,24],[42,20]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"145","lesson":"The empty tomb and appearances of Jesus moved His followers from confusion toward understanding. Resurrection hope belongs with the proclamation of repentance and forgiveness in His name.","question":"How were the disciples directed back to the Scriptures?","wordCount":1994,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90146,"title":"The Stranger on the Shore","subtitle":"Restoration into humble service","text":"John 21","chapters":[[42,21]],"kind":"story","category":"Jesus and His teaching","file":"146","lesson":"Jesus faced Peter’s failure truthfully and renewed his responsibility to care for His people. Grace does not mean hiding failure or ignoring appropriate accountability.","question":"What responsibility accompanied Jesus’s questions about Peter’s love?","wordCount":1249,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90147,"title":"The Church of the First Days","subtitle":"The Spirit forms a witnessing community","text":"Acts 1–2","chapters":[[43,1],[43,2]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"147","lesson":"The first believers depended on God’s promise and responded through teaching, fellowship, prayer and generosity. Spiritual vitality should reach shared life as well as public witness.","question":"What practices shaped the community after Pentecost?","wordCount":793,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90148,"title":"The Man at the Beautiful Gate","subtitle":"A gift that points to Jesus","text":"Acts 3–4","chapters":[[43,3],[43,4]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"148","lesson":"Peter refused to treat the healing as evidence of his own power. Christian service should direct gratitude toward Christ and make room for the truth of the gospel.","question":"To whom did Peter direct the crowd’s attention?","wordCount":1230,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90149,"title":"The Right Way to Give and the Wrong Way","subtitle":"Generosity without a false appearance","text":"Acts 4–5","chapters":[[43,4],[43,5]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"149","lesson":"The contrast between willing giving and deceit shows that God is not impressed by a manufactured reputation. Honesty matters more than appearing sacrificial.","question":"What was wrong with the claim made about the gift?","wordCount":1401,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90150,"title":"Stephen with the Shining Face","subtitle":"Faithful witness at a great cost","text":"Acts 6–7","chapters":[[43,6],[43,7]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"150","lesson":"Stephen served and spoke in the power of the Spirit while opposition grew. His death shows that faithfulness is not measured only by visible safety or public acceptance.","question":"How did Stephen respond toward those killing him?","wordCount":957,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90151,"title":"The Man Reading in the Chariot","subtitle":"Helping another person understand Scripture","text":"Acts 8","chapters":[[43,8]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"151","lesson":"Philip listened to a question, began from the passage and explained the good news about Jesus. Teaching should help people understand rather than display the teacher’s importance.","question":"How did Philip begin with the reader’s actual question?","wordCount":1272,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90152,"title":"The Voice That Spoke to Saul","subtitle":"Grace that changes an enemy’s direction","text":"Acts 9","chapters":[[43,9]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"152","lesson":"Saul’s encounter with the risen Jesus overturned the course of his opposition. Ananias’s obedience also mattered in receiving the person whose reputation made him afraid.","question":"What changed in Saul’s purpose after the encounter?","wordCount":1759,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90153,"title":"What Peter Saw by the Sea","subtitle":"God’s welcome challenges a learned boundary","text":"Acts 10–11","chapters":[[43,10],[43,11]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"153","lesson":"Peter had to recognise the work of God among people he had not been ready to receive. The account points to Christ and the Spirit rather than making social identity a condition of grace.","question":"What did Peter have to acknowledge about God’s action?","wordCount":1710,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90154,"title":"How the Iron Gate Was Opened","subtitle":"Prayer while the outcome is beyond control","text":"Acts 12","chapters":[[43,12]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"154","lesson":"The believers prayed under real danger, and Peter’s release surprised them. The chapter also records James’s death, so it does not make rescue a guarantee for every faithful believer.","question":"Why must both James’s death and Peter’s release be included in the lesson?","wordCount":948,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90155,"title":"The Earliest Missionaries","subtitle":"Witness that remains accountable to the gospel","text":"Acts 13–15","chapters":[[43,13],[43,14],[43,15]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"155","lesson":"The mission crossed boundaries and faced opposition, while the church addressed disputed teaching together. Zeal should remain joined to truth, care and communal discernment.","question":"How did the church address the disputed teaching?","wordCount":1665,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90156,"title":"The Song in the Prison","subtitle":"Mercy in the middle of suffering","text":"Acts 16","chapters":[[43,16]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"156","lesson":"Paul stopped the jailer from harming himself and then spoke the word of the Lord to his household. Prayer and worship were joined to practical concern for another person’s life.","question":"What did Paul do when the jailer was in immediate danger?","wordCount":1727,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90157,"title":"Paul's Speech on the Hill","subtitle":"Speaking truth to the questions people bring","text":"Acts 17","chapters":[[43,17]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"157","lesson":"Paul observed his hearers’ setting but did not simply confirm their beliefs. Faithful explanation should be understandable while remaining truthful about God and the resurrection.","question":"How did Paul connect with his audience and still challenge them?","wordCount":1160,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90158,"title":"Paul at Corinth","subtitle":"Patient service in a mixed response","text":"Acts 18","chapters":[[43,18]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"158","lesson":"Paul’s work at Corinth included friendship, labour, teaching and opposition. Ministry is sustained through ordinary faithfulness as well as public opportunities.","question":"Who shared in the work and encouragement at Corinth?","wordCount":879,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90159,"title":"Paul at Ephesus","subtitle":"A changed allegiance has public effects","text":"Acts 19","chapters":[[43,19]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"159","lesson":"The gospel challenged practices and interests in Ephesus. A response to Christ should reach habits and loyalties rather than remain a private label.","question":"What changes in conduct provoked concern in the city?","wordCount":1548,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90160,"title":"Paul's Last Journey to Jerusalem","subtitle":"Care for people while facing uncertainty","text":"Acts 20–21","chapters":[[43,20],[43,21]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"160","lesson":"Paul’s farewell joined concern for the church with warning and personal example. A faithful servant prepares others to remain watchful rather than dependent on his presence.","question":"What dangers did Paul warn the elders to guard against?","wordCount":1394,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90161,"title":"The Speech on the Stairs","subtitle":"A witness given amid hostility","text":"Acts 21–22","chapters":[[43,21],[43,22]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"161","lesson":"Paul addressed a hostile crowd by telling the truth about his life and calling. Being misunderstood should not remove our commitment to truthful and careful speech.","question":"What did Paul explain about the change in his life?","wordCount":1369,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90162,"title":"Two Years in Prison","subtitle":"Integrity during a long delay","text":"Acts 23–24","chapters":[[43,23],[43,24]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"162","lesson":"Paul’s imprisonment continued despite the absence of a just resolution. Waiting under injustice should not be confused with proof that God has abandoned His servant.","question":"What pressures might have tempted Paul to compromise?","wordCount":1527,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90163,"title":"The Story That Paul Told to the King","subtitle":"A clear testimony before powerful hearers","text":"Acts 25–26","chapters":[[43,25],[43,26]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"163","lesson":"Paul explained his former conduct and the mission entrusted to him. A testimony should give honour to Christ and make the call to repentance understandable.","question":"What did Paul say repentance should lead people to do?","wordCount":1403,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90164,"title":"Paul in the Storm","subtitle":"Courage and practical care in danger","text":"Acts 27","chapters":[[43,27]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"164","lesson":"Paul’s trust in God’s promise did not prevent practical action for the people on the ship. Faith and responsible care for bodily needs belong together.","question":"What practical instructions accompanied Paul’s encouragement?","wordCount":1308,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90165,"title":"How Paul Came to Rome, and How He Lived There","subtitle":"The word continues beyond a servant’s restrictions","text":"Acts 28","chapters":[[43,28]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"165","lesson":"Paul’s limitations did not bring his witness to an end. The open ending directs attention to the continuing proclamation of Christ rather than only to the circumstances of the messenger.","question":"What continued while Paul lived under restriction?","wordCount":1554,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90166,"title":"The Throne of God","subtitle":"Worship before the throne","text":"Revelation 4–7","chapters":[[65,4],[65,5],[65,6],[65,7]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"166","lesson":"The vision directs attention to God’s holiness and the worthiness of the Lamb. Its imagery should lead to worship and faithful endurance, read within the vision’s own explanation.","question":"Who receives honour at the centre of the vision?","wordCount":899,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"},{"id":90167,"title":"The City of God","subtitle":"Hope that leads to faithfulness now","text":"Revelation 21–22","chapters":[[65,21],[65,22]],"kind":"story","category":"The early church and Revelation","file":"167","lesson":"The final vision places the renewal of creation and God’s presence with His people before the reader. Christian hope should sustain holiness and endurance rather than an escape from present responsibility.","question":"How should the promised future shape present conduct?","wordCount":907,"source":"Hurlbut 1904"}];
